-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                               MILAWA README

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Milawa is a "self-verifying" theorem prover for an ACL2-like logic, developed
by Jared Davis for his Ph.D. dissertation.  For an overview of Milawa, see doc.lisp, which is rendered in the ACL2 Community
Books manual under @(see milawa).

This directory contains the Milawa source code, which is part of the ACL2
Community Books project (https://github.com/acl2/acl2).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                             BUILD INSTRUCTIONS

Milawa is not built during the "make" of the ordinary ACL2 Community Books,
because building it takes several hours, requires a lot of memory and hard disk
space, and needs a Common Lisp with generous stack and heap settings.  See
BUILD-PROCEDURE.md in this directory for the setup that is known to work.

Instead, to build Milawa, you should:

1. Build ACL2 with CCL, the default and recommended builder:

     $ cd <acl2-root>
     $ make LISP=ccl

   SBCL also works, but level10 needs an SBCL runtime with a larger binding
   stack, so it requires a custom SBCL build; see BUILD-PROCEDURE.md section
   3.1.  That binding-stack requirement is on the ACL2 proof builder, not on
   the standalone kernel: CMUCL, CLISP, SCL, Allegro CL, and ECL are also
   supported builders for the kernel image itself (see below).

2. Generate the proofs (this is the long step).  Run it serially:

     $ cd books/projects/milawa/ACL2
     $ make ACL2=<acl2-root>/saved_acl2

   Do not use -j here: several books in a level prove the same theorem name
   and write the same proof file, so parallel certification can leave a proof
   that does not match the level's .events manifest (BUILD-PROCEDURE.md §4).

A successful build should result in:

  - Several new ACL2 images in acl2-books/milawa/ACL2/acl2-images/,
    including one called "user-symmetry"

  - ACL2 certificates (.cert) or "ACL2 Milawa Provisional Certificates"
    (.mpcert) for files throughout the milawa/ACL2 directory

  - Thousands of proof files under milawa/Proofs/<level>/<book>/, one
    directory per book that certified proofs at that level.

These proof files are the "boostrapping proofs" that can now be checked by the
Milawa kernel.  There are many options here: Milawa has two kernels:

  - The original kernel, a Common Lisp program that has historically been run
    successfully on at least CCL, SBCL, CMUCL, and (for the very patient)
    CLISP, on certain platforms.  In this case, the proof files can be
    processed directly.

  - The new kernel, either a Common Lisp or Jitawa Lisp program, that can be
    run on at least CCL, SBCL, and Jitawa (a verified Lisp by Magnus Myreen).
    In this case, the proof files first need to be further collected and
    compressed.

Magnus Myreen has used the HOL4 system to prove an unbelievably impressive
theorem: if you run the simplified Milawa kernel on the Jitawa runtime on an
64-bit X86 system, it can only accept theorems of formulas that are
semantically true.  This theorem connects the logical semantics, down through
the kernel's source code, through the Jitawa Lisp implementation, down to a
formal HOL model of the x86 machine code!

To check the proofs with the standalone kernel, run this from the directory
containing this README:

     $ make                        # CCL: build base.<ext>, then check
     $ make LISP=sbcl              # SBCL fallback (see above)
     $ make LISP=cmucl             # likewise for CMUCL, CLISP, SCL, Allegro CL:
     $ make LISP=clisp             # any Lisp milawa.lsp can save an image in
     $ make LISP=scl               # is a builder here, and milawa-<lisp> runs
     $ make LISP=acl               # the image it produced
     $ make LISP=ecl               # (ECL images are directories; see below)

That is a wrapper around

     $ ./final-checks.sh <lisp> <image-extension>

e.g. `./final-checks.sh ccl ccl-linux-aarch64` (the GNUmakefile derives the
platform-specific extension from `uname` and prints it).

which replays Proofs/<level>.events through the kernel, one level at a time.
It is resumable: a level whose image already exists is skipped, and
`make check-level L=<level>` redoes a single level.  BUILD-PROCEDURE.md
section 7 has the details.

ECL cannot save Lisp heap images, so under `make LISP=ecl` the images are
directories named like the others (e.g. "base.ecl-linux-aarch64") containing
a `state.lisp` that records the checker's history.  `milawa-ecl` restores
that history and then runs the checker; everything else, including
`final-checks.sh`, works unchanged.  Install ECL first, e.g. `apt install
ecl`, or build it from source (https://ecl.common-lisp.dev/); the fork at
https://gitlab.com/jimwhite/ecl is the one to use if changes turn out to be
needed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                               FILE GUIDE

Files

  milawa.lsp
    -- The core proof checker (complete and stand-alone), as of my
       dissertation

  milawa2.lsp
    -- Simplified version of milawa.lsp, for use with Magnus's
       verified Jitawa Lisp runtime

  GNUmakefile
    -- Builds base.<ext> and runs the final checks

  final-checks.sh
    -- Replays Proofs/<level>.events through the standalone kernel

  milawa-sbcl, milawa-ccl, milawa-cmucl, milawa-clisp, milawa-acl, milawa-scl
    -- Launch a saved image under the corresponding Lisp.  Heap and stack
       sizes come from the environment; see the top of each script.

  milawa-ecl
    -- Launches an ECL image, which is a directory holding state.lisp rather
       than a saved image (ECL cannot dump heap images).

  BUILD-PROCEDURE.md
    -- Detailed build procedure, as measured on a known-good setup

  report-time.rb
    -- Tool for seeing which events took longest to process

  summarize-sizes.rb
    -- Tool for summarizing proof sizes per level


Subdirectories:

  ACL2/
    -- Main directory: ACL2 proofs + User Interface + Bootstrapping
    -- Basically everything you want is here

  Proofs/
    -- Proof files generated during the bootstrapping process, in
       Proofs/<level>/<book>/, plus the <level>.events manifest for each
       level.  These are the files for our "final checks".


